<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Feb 18, 2023">
<CPscript title="Function Addition in CirclePack" level="min" screenmode="map" >
<description>Circle packings provide an approach to analytic function theory, but it is geometric --- algebraic manipulations are hardly in its vocabulary. However, command 'h_g_add', motivated by the harmonic function work with 'h_g_bar', provides interesting (and unexplored) experimental openings. Perhaps there are discoveries awaiting you!</description>
<text> The script "HGintro.xmd" introduced the basics of discrete harmonic functions using the specialized 'h_g_bar' command. We start with a specific example of its use as motivation and then move on to the analogous analytic version 'h_g_add', which algebraically adds discrete analytic functions.

We do not exploit this topic here --- since the circles themselves are not carrying the geometric information, this is not circle packing, per se. But there are intriguing questions that even these simple experiments raise, so you might find yourself drawn into the topic. With more varied trials, you might find the circles still working for us somehow. Give it a try! </text>
<Section title="Harmonic Motivation">
<text> Discrete analytic functions h and g share the same complex. Here h will be the identity mapping, with image packing in p0, while g is the discrete analogue of -z^2/2, with image in p1. (Check out the construction of g to confirm this and explain why symmetry makes the image look like a single-sheeted packing.) </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" tooltip="Start over, h and g each the identity function" iconname="klines.png">act 0;Cleanse;infile_read dom.p;max_pack;geom_to_e;color -c 20 b;rotate -.5;Disp -w -c -cf b;copy 1;act 1;set_vlist z 0.0 0.0;set_aim 4.0 vlist;repack;layout;rotate .5;color -c 20 b;Disp -w -c -cf b;Map 0 1; </cmd>
<text> The next command will apply 'h_g_bar' and then shift the screens to show g on the left and f = h + conj(g) on the right. This example mimics the harmonic function z-(1/2)(conj(z))^2 (related to, but not the same functions as Figure 4.16 in the book).  If you look at the command you will see that we rotated p0 by -pi/2 and p1 by pi/2 to account for the y-axis normalization that is standard in CirclePack. </text>
<cmd iconname="netbeans.png">act 1;[d];Map 1 2 </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Analytic Comparison">
<text> For comparison, we can consider the analytic combination f = h+g (note that we are not conjugating g). Start as before with h and g. </text>
<cmd iconname="centrejust.png">act 0;Cleanse;infile_read dom.p;max_pack;geom_to_e;rotate -.5;color -c 20 b;Disp -w -c -cf b;copy 1;act 1;set_vlist z 0.0 0.0;set_aim 4.0 vlist;repack;layout;rotate .5;Disp -w -c -cf b; Map 0 1; </cmd>
<text> As with 'h_g_bar', the command 'h_g_add' adds the centers of euclidean packings in p0 and p1 and puts the results in p2. (Compare this to Figure 4.13 in the book, created by the applet "ComplexTool". If you look at the command details, you will see that we have rotated h by -pi/2 and g by +pi/2; this adjusts for our normalizations and puts the negative sign in front of z^2/2.) </text>
<cmd iconname="button_ok.png">h_g_add;act 2;Disp -w -f;Map 1 2; </cmd>
<text> You should compare this to Figure 4.13 in the book, created by the applet "ComplexTool". If we transfer a line-spoke grid from the domain, p0,  the result looks especially close. </text>
<cmd iconname="dnd_multi.png">Map 0 2;set_grid -p0;Disp -p0 -bc5 t3;Disp -p2 -w -f -bc5t3; </cmd>
<text> The 'h_g_add' command does add the radii from p0 and p1 to give radii for p2, but only for lack of any more reasonable way to associate some radii. If you plot the circles in p2 you will see that they don't form a packing. The circles are not doing the work for you in p2. </text>
<cmd iconname="psi.png">disp -p2 -w -c -f; </cmd>
<text> Out of curiosity, suppose we compare the image in p2 to a circle packing associated with f = z-(1/2)z^2. For this construction note that f' = 1-z. We enter this derivative in the "Function" window and use 'set_ratio', along with a rotation for normalization. (You can see more about this construction method in "RatioBuild.xmd".) </text>
<cmd iconname="mozilla.png">act 1;set_function_text 1-z;set_aim -d;set_ratio 0 1;repack;layout;norm_scale -h 115 97;set_screen -a;disp -w -c -f -et8c20 b;disp -p2 -et8c20 b;Map 1 2; </cmd>
<text> Perhaps we should not be surprised that these are so close --- but the fideility is pretty impressive, isn't it? CirclePack has a command that is useful for such comparisons: 'disp -q{.} ...' will display objects from one packing on the screen of another. Let's apply that here, first displaying the faces of p2 as usual on its own screen, then overlaying with the faces of p1 (in red). </text>
<cmd iconname="button_ok.png">act 1;disp -p2 -w -f;disp -q2 -fc195; </cmd>
<text> This suggests some confidence in the reliability of discrete function addition. Clearly, however, more experiments are in order. One starting point: repeat this script with more generic domain packings in place of 'dom.p'. Good luck! </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="d" mnemonic="d" tooltip="apply h_g_add, results in p2" iconname="network.png">act 2;h_g_bar;color -f 20 j;disp -w -f -ffc j;set_disp_text -w -f -ffc j; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
<CPdata>
